\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161028" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161028" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {"question" "q-161028" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161028" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2d95ce1c0b844b49000b05ef44ef0da8b516740804a8e6bd275a1f9ca15b2ed0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/539932b6d0bc70809848bf1690df34dc56ebb569dc4877c231bd08e8698c5764.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a63ab6b0cb9e525901e8173ae54afb89530ae96342975731b76e51b2a51d752e.png"] ["text" "The image is the empty set"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161028" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8afe0884ccf1e62ffdfef6cab4626bc59b3138aa5cd26f855980c88971753447.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b836799bc512db70d2ce5fcfe70db2af3b2572d584d317fa5ed515b363a11ddc.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point $(0,\\,0),$ which is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{5},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies in the circle with radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {"question" "q-161035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/708bf4df2d45383b0dd367f5bfe3dc9e260a17cfefe0566e2b6d37dec98af2ef.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $0\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})),\\,0\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {"question" "q-161026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/13810a874a27ea2db773ed90fa02f5b1b833d90372b38a3273f416b30d9a1a1f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/cbcba98cd11ce740be941848d0d9d4524c8cbc58d6de7aee0cfb2f7421c457db.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/deafbae15e6d30f175122d3662397f05398d31579b80520648accaf1fff93e85.png"] ["text" "The image is the empty set"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3621e70552c3891f43521e74704d5db3f276f9928669390d688cca1bc3449217.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c8ad4cf64e9ea17b225373d8ed238c8588c0b6f45577d2ca15b22ede5e34d7d8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {"question" "q-160914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0799c923b5e7061f8a6ec1faf9ed8b73038dfe4c1b63607a8e037d66d6ce88fd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/25ec9c36f1e6637a0034cdae142977b4a4b5c3237471ef93f4bf55c6f4ea86b7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/39ba02dcd51d54bf28a84e19786d763c201858979c45f65a11069b7ba4d0584d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5263c0c82be5ab6b0290e5017943c7c2584d3bd23783c5420e38af2df618f9b0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5adf324eba50123bd9a6dc3f27be38a7db0b4835bac8479e705f696abcf404b4.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/39ba02dcd51d54bf28a84e19786d763c201858979c45f65a11069b7ba4d0584d.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_