ure-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff3