" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f436ee381cafdb41a4a8df2bc68c06751d7d0a6d55641303caf04dcb4cab6619.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {"question" "q-160912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0c093c8b5b55bcf5c50b0fc6c60d47256c9aaf86b8114ba29d6cef5def8cd310.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/36cd7eaa8f2be8e3c8be883f7de52c2ea8bb87b2ac40b6ae0f9de22598d53ef6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/993c8929f019f9753f8b9e0b6662248bf9f41b8f743d423ef11c4838922de17c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ce5b81c795019bb779f766d70a42872ef15aac38440a4c38876c76842b3a0c69.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c86259b973101d17d732f4ac8c29a1b61de9fadfc67424a2ab6d28a2ceaa9a01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {"question" "q-160925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1c9d7de1e895affd9f75deda09fe4b3243ea39a04b47198309e0b52c6197b2f8.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2e8246c7764ee6a059da534fa078f3d3c7caebfbc55554be2da63002da2007bc.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2eb8b5f7ee3fe1b85960f1d6c87a2aabf58aae6f97394b2c7145cc98b39708dc.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/37bccae9033e5eac9a48f8d3d570d69d1a382f26646617665c75f32a9ac7ab4e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81ccd8830bcafa56065d5f7481e0d0b1dc114d0cd67a9d04220c8bb4459968d3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=0$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/968e682dbaa9d8bdb95808e609a1dba6c68e8e12d9cae1abb59755b5ec640286.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-160951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d0b12b354212cb5afd066e632738204f9acafb010ccb8683e448c306ff5704c"} {