demy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c9cd25963ec1ae28860b821825597ca16074e3b0231bd83da7505d37405c23c2.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/88d