f227f5a1844628d5f34fb613644b43d2.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "q-161118" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/state.json" "file_sha256" "7514841bab25e28fa54563ba7ce72b6b8a8ea77b5729d54494a380b378b1e7b9"} {"question" "e-11153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/93c82014d308d19ebfc10e7046ac1e8b6cf78017b6bc10e0671f43d22aac996a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the right side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-11153.json" "file_sha256" "d23211df803ab857854189cf94d1f5f35793174d5b794d4f858a038e9b227136"} {"question" "e-11153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u = 2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u = 2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8b14a175f7931dda3e3b6afd97c5219c390a0674f147289a257e0e655dcc11f1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-11153.json" "file_sha256" "d23211df803ab857854189cf94d1f5f35793174d5b794d4f858a038e9b227136"} {"question" "e-12363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/86a240b5c7c1fc945e12da274be7e9cac14c1b5a9add004d52d72390afb39aff.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-12363.json" "file_sha256" "2a9a823997b0d071aef00d380eb52365a9c64db5a4a008e63ad50b9369da5c55"} {"question" "e-12363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-12363.json" "file_sha256" "2a9a823997b0d071aef00d380eb52365a9c64db5a4a008e63ad50b9369da5c55"} {"question" "e-12340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/6515550b9a280f423cdc2a2e89fd83014733c0783fdd4b2b2966e6a4dd37492a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-12340.json" "file_sha256" "6d5923c896db7fd17178131f2de048d588986dc83fe8203ded0910c136bdd0aa"} {"question" "e-12340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-12340.json" "file_sha256" "6d5923c896db7fd17178131f2de048d588986dc83fe8203ded0910c136bdd0aa"} {"question" "e-12332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1a869685bd7163515672154b20c2a41a50b5df320bae30e650d7a6d0b7d44ee1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v) , \\, y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y= u \\sin v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $\\Delta$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Sketch the region $\\Delta.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-12332.json" "file_sha256" "27182a7dd0d8acf34221e992bbb788a9cec23adf56dee258ae12663483a2fee0"} {"question" "e-12332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c83bd9d913d4959ad275ab4420b647cd030fff45438f59d026e3658bd146a1ee.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/example-12332.json" "file_sha256" "27182a7dd0d8acf34221e992bbb788a9cec23adf56dee258ae12663483a2fee0"}] "usage" {"e-12332" [] "q-160414" [] "q-161161" [] "e-12340" [] "e-12363" [] "e-11153" []} "basis" 1694 "retractions" [[17592186198504 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-160414.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 1 \\le u \\le \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{5} \\le v \\le \\frac{π}{3}}$,\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (u\\cos v, u\\sin v)$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$."] [17592186198527 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/q-161161.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T$, given by\n\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\n\nwhere\n\n$x = u\\cos v, y = u\\sin v$,\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D$. Which of the following could be the region $Δ$?"] [17592186197939 :question/problem "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\left\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/403cdd2076f477fc63ccb7d639c01dd8.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v)$.\n\nDetermine the image of the right side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$."] [17592186197939 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u = 2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u = 2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (u\\cos v, u\\sin v) \\\\\n&= (2\\cos v, 2\\sin v), \\frac{π}{4} \\le v \\le \\frac{2π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane.\nThe arc lies in the first and second quadrants between the polar rays\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/3fdee4f64327a57e730e6e81fa4f0aad.png)"] [17592186197954 :question/problem "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\left\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/ab40ef68e1926a48a712dca5721d947a.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v)$.\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$."] [17592186197954 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1 \\leq u \\leq 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ninto our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x, y) &= T(u, v) \\\\\n&= (u\\cos v, u\\sin v) \\\\\n&= (u\\cos (\\frac{4π}{3}), u\\sin (\\frac{4π}{3})), 1 \\le u \\le 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwith the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/284be16ac10ed45614ba06b8ec36be32.png)"] [17592186197947 :question/problem "Consider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$\\Delta = \\bigg\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}$,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/30b7aa3de0ba4ae739a3cc65f315309c.png)\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v)$.\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$."] [17592186197947 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3$. Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (u\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}), u\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}))$.\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O$.\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3$. Substituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (u\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}), u\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}))$.\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O$.\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$. Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (\\cos v, \\sin v)$.\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$.\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$. Substituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n$(x, y) = (3\\cos v, 3\\sin v)$.\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between\n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3$, and\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$,\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/6e571d4127648d98fe51a7671f53c221.png)"] [17592186197945 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/a62cb26f3c3f7dc30da2c43f45be00a6.png)\n\n$\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v), \\, y(u,v))$,\n\nwhere\n\n$x = u \\cos v, \\qquad y= u \\sin v$,\n\nmaps the region $\\Delta$ from the $uv$-plane to $D$. Sketch the region $\\Delta$."] [17592186197945 :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3$, and\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$,\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4135/Source/Images/818d8bbe7fb53985dca3540435950c04.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186193926 17592186193927 17592186193928 17592186193929 17592186193930 17592186194064 17592186194065 17592186194066 17592186194067 17592186194068] "immutable_fields" [[17592186193925 :answer-field/correct] [17592186193925 :answer-field/id] [17592186193925 :answer-field/key] [17592186193925 :answer-field/type] [17592186194063 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194063 :answer-field/id] [17592186194063 :answer-field/key] [17592186194063 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
